<p>
After reading the sad news that <a href="https://irreal.org/blog/?p=13775">Chris Wellons is abandoning Emacs and the Emacs packages he developed</a> one of my first concerns was for <a href="https://github.com/skeeto/elfeed">Elfeed</a>. It&rsquo;s the RSS reader that I depend on everyday for my research and news curation. Wellons will be missed for many reasons but his stewardship of Elfeed is the most important one to me.
</p>
<p>
I&rsquo;m all but certain that Wellons will find good hands in which to entrust the future of Elfeed but as I learned in the Boy Scouts, it pays to <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jlSXkq2FUSA">be prepared</a>. Serendipitously, I stumbled upon <a href="https://davemq.github.io/emacs/gnus/url/2026/04/29/visiting-urls-from-gnus-summary.html">this post from Dave&rsquo;s Blog</a> that mentions he moved from Elfeed to <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gnus">Gnus</a>. The post is about a solution he found for following links referred to in the RSS summary but I was more interested in his use of Gnus in place of Elfeed for RSS.
</p>
<p>
I&rsquo;ve considered, off and on, moving to Gnus but I&rsquo;m really happy with <a href="https://djcbsoftware.nl/code/mu/mu4e/index.html">mu4e</a> for email and elfeed for RSS and Network News is, sadly, no longer important so moving to Gnus didn&rsquo;t make much sense to me. Now, unfortunately, I may have to think about it. Gnus is a powerful complicated program and it seems a shame to use it just for RSS but if worse comes to worst it&rsquo;s nice to have a mature and reliable fallback available.
</p>
<p>
As I said, I&rsquo;m pretty sure Elfeed won&rsquo;t have any problems finding a new maintainer who will sustain its high quality but it&rsquo;s nice to know that if I need it, there&rsquo;s a viable, Emacs-based solution available.</p>
